course: dictionary
lesson: 2385
title: Curiosity: the wish to know
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Curiosity is a noun. It means a strong wish to know or learn about something. When you wonder why the sky is blue or how a bee finds its flower, that wish is curiosity. Good readers and scientists have lots of it.
explain: The word can also mean a strange or rare object that people find interesting. Its plural is curiosities. Look for clues in the sentence to see which meaning is used. «Curiosity found smooth, rounded pebbles like those in a stream» is about the NASA rover, so the capital C shows it is a name.
explain: Curiosity is praised in school and science because it leads to questions and answers. «Curiosity is how we learn: asking "why?" and then looking for the answer yourself makes you a strong learner.» But curiosity must be careful. Do not touch unknown things, medicines or wild animals just to find out what they are.
explain: Curiosity can also lead a character into trouble, as in a story. «Burning with curiosity, Alice dashes after him and jumps down a rabbit-hole without once thinking how she will get out again.» Look for phrases like «out of curiosity» and «full of curiosity» when you read.
fact: Curiosity means a strong wish to know or learn about something.
fact: A curiosity can also be a strange or rare object that people find interesting.
fact: Curiosity is also the name of a NASA rover that explores Mars.
fact: Curiosity needs care: ask an adult before you touch anything unknown, such as medicines or wild animals.
fact: «Out of curiosity» means because you want to know, and «full of curiosity» means very eager to find out.
exercise: «Curiosity is how we learn: asking "why?" and then looking for the answer yourself makes you a strong learner.» In this sentence, what is curiosity? | *A strong wish to know or learn | Being very tired | A pebble found in a stream | A kind of medicine
exercise: In «Curiosity found smooth, rounded pebbles like those in a stream», what is Curiosity? | A strong wish to learn | A strange, rare object in a museum | *A NASA rover exploring Mars | A stream full of water
exercise: «Memes and comedy can spark curiosity, but you need to know the real story to understand the joke.» What should you do when curiosity is sparked? | Stop asking questions | Touch the unknown thing | Believe everything you see | *Find the real story first
test: «Burning with curiosity, Alice dashes after him and jumps down a rabbit-hole.» What does burning with curiosity show? | Alice is cold and bored | Alice is lost in a wood | Alice is very angry with the rabbit | *Alice is extremely eager to find out what is happening
test: Which action shows safe curiosity about an unknown object? | *Asking an adult what it is before you touch it | Touching a wild animal to see how it feels | Tasting an unknown medicine to find out what it is | Kicking it to see if it moves
exam: «Languages keep changing, borrowing and playing, so the best language skill is curiosity: notice odd words, ask what they mean and enjoy how people talk.» According to this sentence, what is the best language skill? | Silence | Memorising long lists | Speed | *Curiosity
